],["hs","ws"],["at","South should, therefore, think about the worst case and assume that West has the @SQ and that a trump trick will have to be lost. "],["pg",""],["ls","wsnsss"],["hs","nc"],["at","Using this reasoning (called 'second-degree assumption' by ^*BReese^*N) South can deduce the correct play in clubs. "],["pg",""],["ls","nc"],["hs","wh"],["nt","West's lead of the @HK strongly suggests that he was dealt the @HA as well. "],["pg",""],["hs","ws"],["ls","wh"],["at","Under the assumption that West has the @SQ (otherwise the contract will be easy), "],["pg",""],["ls","ws"],["md","0,ca,,cq"],["hs","wc"],["at","West cannot have the @CA. "],["pg",""],["hs","wc"],["at","That would give West 13 HCP, but West passed in first seat. "],["pg",""],["md","0,cq,,ca"],["hs","nc"],["at","It is possible that West has the @CQ so South should play the @C10 or @CJ from the dummy. "],["pg",""],["ls","ncwc"],["pc","cj"],["md","0,c7c8h8,,cqsq4"],["hs","Ec"],["nt","On the actual layout, South's club play is irrelevant as East, who was dealt both the @CA and @CQ, wins the next 2 tricks in that suit. "],["pg",""],["ls","ec"],["pc","cqc"],["pg",""],["pc","caccc"],["pg",""],["hs","ehss"],["at","When East returns a heart, South should be careful to ruff with a middle trump, preserving the @S7. "],["pg",""],["ls","sseh"],["pc","hs9hh"],["pg",""],["hs","nsss"],["nt","South must now avoid losing a trump trick and will have to hope that the @SQ is with East, but it would be wrong to lay down a top honor before crossing to dummy for a finesse. "],["pg",""],["hs","ws"],["md","0,sq,,s5"],["at","To do so would gain if West were dealt a singleton @SQ, "],["pg",""],["md","0,s5,,sq"],["hs","es"],["at","but if West has a singleton spade it is 4 times as likely to be a low card. Laying down a high trump would be fatal on the above layout of the East-West cards. "],["pg",""],["ls","nsssesws"],["hs","ndsd"],["nt","Instead, South should cross to the dummy in diamonds "],["pg",""],["ls","ndsd"],["pc","dddtd"],["pg",""],["hs","nsesss"],["at","and take a first round spade finesse by passing dummy's @S8. "],["pg",""],["ls","nsesss"],["pc","s8sss"],["pg",""],["hs","esnsss"],["at","Thanks to South's thoughtful ruff with a middle trump on the second round of hearts, the lead is still in the dummy and the trump finesse can be repeated. "],["pg",""],["ls","nsss"],["hs","ndsd"],["at","By playing in this manner South has to cross to the dummy only once in diamonds, minimizing the risk of a defensive ruff in that suit."],["",""]]}}